[Repertoire "black-slav"]
[Side "black"]
[Title "The Slav setup"]
[Tagline "Same ideas as the Caro, just against 1.d4: ...d5, ...c6, ...Bf5, ...e6."]
[Chapter "Slav (2.c4)"]
[Plans "Finish with ...Nbd7 and ...Qe7, then free your game with ...e5, or ...c5 if the center is quiet. The bishop lives on f5; if a knight jumps to h4 or e5, drop it to g6 and take any open h-file as a gift. If White ever takes on d5 and you recapture with the e-pawn, expect the minority attack (b4-b5): answer with ...Ne4 and play on the kingside."]

{The Queen's Gambit. You defend d5 with ...c6 and keep your bishop free. It's the Caro-Kann's cousin from the other side of the family.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 {[%cal Gc6d5]} 3. Nf3
( 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3
  ( 4. Nf3 dxc4 5. a4 Bf5 )
  ( 4. cxd5 cxd5 5. Bf4 Nc6 6. e3 Bf5 7. Nf3 e6 {Exchange Slav: symmetrical, calm. Develop and don't blunder.} )
  Bf5 5. Nf3 e6 )
( 3. cxd5 cxd5 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Bf4 Nc6 6. e3 Bf5 7. Nf3 e6 )
( 3. e3 Nf6 4. Nf3
  ( 4. Nc3 Bf5 5. Nf3 e6 )
  Bf5 )
Nf6 4. Nc3
( 4. e3 Bf5 {Outside the chain before ...e6. [%cal Gc8f5]} 5. Nc3
  ( 5. Bd3 Bxd3 6. Qxd3 e6 )
  ( 5. cxd5 cxd5 6. Qb3 {Hits b7, which your bishop left behind. [%cal Rb3b7]} Qb6 {Trade queens and the problem goes away.} 7. Qxb6 axb6 )
  ( 5. Qb3 Qb6 )
  e6 6. Nh4 {Wants your bishop. [%cal Rh4f5]}
  ( 6. Be2 Nbd7 7. O-O Bd6 )
  Bg6 7. Nxg6 hxg6 {You get an open h-file in return. [%cal Gh8h2]} )
dxc4 {Take the pawn now that White can't easily win it back.} 5. a4 {Stops ...b5. [%csl Rb5]}
( 5. e3 b5 {Hold the pawn. [%cal Gb5c4]} 6. a4 b4 7. Na2 e6 8. Bxc4 Be7 9. O-O O-O )
( 5. e4 b5 6. e5 Nd5 7. a4 e6 {Hold c4 and get that knight to a great square.} )
Bf5 {There's that bishop again. [%cal Gc8f5]} 6. e3 e6 7. Bxc4 Bb4 8. O-O O-O {Main line Slav. Everything developed, nothing to fear.}

[Chapter "vs the London (2.Bf4)"]
[Plans "With the bishops traded, White's attacking plans are gone and the game is about the center and queenside. Castle, then free yourself with ...e5 (the queen on d6 loves backing it) or go ...c5 and ...Qb6 against b2. Don't let a knight sit on e5 forever: trade it off with ...Nxe5."]

{You used to play this. Now you're on the other side. Mirror it: ...Bf5 trades off White's best attacking piece, the d3 bishop.}
1. d4 d5 2. Bf4
( 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Bf4
  ( 3. c4 c6 {Slav.} )
  ( 3. e3 Bf5 4. Bd3
    ( 4. c4 c6 {Slav again.} )
    e6 5. Bxf5 exf5 6. Qd3 Qc8 {Defends f5 and b7.} )
  c6 4. e3 Bf5 )
( 2. e3 Nf6 3. Bd3 c6 4. Nf3 Bg4 )
( 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 c6 4. e3 Bf5 )
Nf6 3. e3
( 3. Nf3 c6 4. e3 Bf5 )
c6 4. Nf3
( 4. c4 e6 5. Nc3 Nbd7 6. Nf3 Bd6 )
( 4. Bd3 Bg4 {Annoy the queen before White's setup is finished. [%cal Gg4d1]} 5. Nf3
  ( 5. f3 Bh5 6. Ne2 e6 )
  e6 6. Nbd2 Bd6 )
Bf5 5. Bd3
( 5. c4 e6 6. Nc3 Nbd7 )
( 5. Nbd2 e6 6. c3 Nbd7 7. Be2 Bd6 )
( 5. Be2 e6 6. O-O Nbd7 )
Bxd3 6. Qxd3 e6 7. O-O Bd6 {Trade the other bishop too. [%cal Gd6f4]} 8. Bxd6 Qxd6 9. Nbd2 Nbd7 {Equal, solid, and White's London plans are gone. [%cal Ge6e5,Gc6c5]}

[Chapter "vs 1.c4, 1.Nf3 and friends"]
[Plans "Your pawns on d5 and e6 are the point: White is sitting back, so take the center. Castle short, play ...Qe7 and ...Re8, then push ...e5 for a big d5-e5 duo. If White takes on d5, recapture so a pawn stays in the middle. Mind the b2 bishop on the long diagonal; don't open it toward your king."]

{Flank openings. Same recipe: ...d5, ...Nf6, ...c6, ...Bf5 or ...e6. It works against basically everything.}
1. c4
( 1. Nf3 d5 2. g3
  ( 2. d4 Nf6 )
  ( 2. c4 c6 )
  Nf6 3. Bg2 c6 4. O-O Bf5 5. d3 e6 )
